export const CASE_STUDY_HTML = "<p class=\"cx-label\"><strong>Example teardown (illustrative numbers).</strong> This is a worked example of our method on a typical pipeline. It is not a client result. We will replace it with real, approved client numbers after our first audit.</p><h2>How a $6,800/mo agent pipeline gets to $2,150/mo in 5 days</h2><p class=\"cx-sub\">An illustrative teardown of a multi-agent pipeline for a 50-person engineering team. Same method we use in the 5-Day Sprint.</p><h3>The setup (illustrative)</h3>\n<p>A 50-person engineering team runs a multi-agent pipeline:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>planner agent</strong> on a large model breaks down tickets and coordinates the others.</li>\n<li>A <strong>retrieval and summarizer agent</strong> pulls docs and code context.</li>\n<li>A <strong>code review agent</strong> reviews every pull request.</li>\n<li>A <strong>test and eval runner</strong> checks agent output on each change.</li>\n<li>Retries are handled by a wrapper with <strong>no cap</strong>. No per-run token budget. No alerting.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Monthly spend: <strong>$6,800</strong>, up month over month. Nobody owns the number.</p>\n<h3>What the teardown found</h3>\n<p><strong>1. Loop detection</strong><br>\nThe planner sometimes rejects a tool result and calls the same tool with near-identical input. With no step limit, some runs go 30+ steps before timing out. Overnight batch jobs make it worse because no one is watching.<br>\n<em>Fix:</em> step limits per run, a loop detector that stops a run when the same tool is called with near-identical input 3 times, and an alert to a human.</p>\n<p><strong>2. Redundant uncached calls</strong><br>\nThe retrieval agent fetches and summarizes the same docs for every ticket. The same summaries are generated many times a day.<br>\n<em>Fix:</em> a response cache keyed on document version, so a doc is summarized once until it changes.</p>\n<p><strong>3. Prompt caching</strong><br>\nEvery planner call resends a long system prompt, full tool schemas and a style guide. That prefix is identical across calls and paid at full input price each time.<br>\n<em>Fix:</em> restructure prompts so the stable prefix comes first and turn on provider prompt caching. Cached input is billed at a fraction of normal input price.</p>\n<p><strong>4. Model routing and downgrade</strong><br>\nThe large model handles every step, including classifying tickets, formatting output and summarizing docs.<br>\n<em>Fix:</em> route simple steps to a small model. Keep the large model for planning and hard reviews. Code review starts on a mid-size model and escalates only on large or risky diffs.</p>\n<p><strong>5. Context trimming</strong><br>\nAgents pass full conversation history and whole files to each other.<br>\n<em>Fix:</em> pass summaries and diffs, cap history length, and drop tool output the next step does not need.</p>\n<p><strong>6. Retry caps and guardrails</strong><br>\nFailed calls retry without limit and without backoff.<br>\n<em>Fix:</em> max 2 retries with backoff, per-run and per-day token budgets, spend alerts per agent, and a hard stop that pages a human instead of spending.</p>\n<h3>Before / after by workload (illustrative)</h3>\n<table class=\"cx-table\"><caption>Example teardown (illustrative numbers)</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Workload</th>\n<th>Before (/mo)</th>\n<th>After (/mo)</th>\n<th>Main fixes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Planner agent</td>\n<td>$2,400</td>\n<td>$900</td>\n<td>Prompt caching, context trimming</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Code review agent</td>\n<td>$1,500</td>\n<td>$600</td>\n<td>Model routing with escalation, caching</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Retrieval and summarizer</td>\n<td>$1,300</td>\n<td>$350</td>\n<td>Response cache, small model</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Loops and uncapped retries</td>\n<td>$1,100</td>\n<td>$50</td>\n<td>Loop detection, retry caps, budgets</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Test and eval runner</td>\n<td>$500</td>\n<td>$250</td>\n<td>Run on changed paths only, small model</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Total</strong></td>\n<td><strong>$6,800</strong></td>\n<td><strong>$2,150</strong></td>\n<td><strong>$4,650/mo saved (about 68%)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Savings by lever (illustrative)</h3>\n<table class=\"cx-table\"><caption>Example teardown (illustrative numbers)</caption>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Lever</th>\n<th>Monthly savings</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Prompt caching</td>\n<td>$1,200</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Model routing and downgrade</td>\n<td>$1,150</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Loop detection and retry caps</td>\n<td>$1,050</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Removing redundant uncached calls</td>\n<td>$700</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Context trimming</td>\n<td>$550</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Total</strong></td>\n<td><strong>$4,650</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>The 5 days (illustrative)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Day 1:</strong> Pull billing exports and traces. Map every agent to its model, volume and cost.</li>\n<li><strong>Day 2:</strong> Trace loops and repeat calls. Size each one in dollars.</li>\n<li><strong>Day 3:</strong> Ship prompt caching, the response cache and context trimming.</li>\n<li><strong>Day 4:</strong> Ship model routing, retry caps, budgets, loop detection and alerts.</li>\n<li><strong>Day 5:</strong> Measure against the baseline, confirm output quality on real tasks, hand off the runbook.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>What this means for you</h3>\n<p>Your numbers will be different. Some stacks have more waste, some less. The method is the same, and the teardown shows your number before you pay anything.</p>\n<p><em>Illustrative numbers for a typical pipeline, not a client result. Real client numbers will replace this example after our first audit, with the client's permission.</em></p>\n<p class=\"cx-ctas\"><a class=\"btn btn-primary\" href=\"/send-your-bill/#bill\" data-ph-cta=\"send-bill\">Send Your AI Bill</a> <a class=\"btn btn-outline\" href=\"/go/triage?from=case-study1-2\" data-ph-cta=\"triage\" data-ph-from=\"case-study1-2\">Book a Free Triage</a></p>\n";
